course: dictionary
lesson: 10674
title: Unnamed people and things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unnamed means without a name, or with its name not given. «An unnamed source close to the team said the star would leave.» We never learn who the source is, because their name is kept secret. The word tells you the name is missing on purpose or simply not known.
explain: In news, reporters often leave out names to protect people, so you will see «an unnamed man» in a story. Read carefully. Does the story tell you who said it? If not, the speaker is unnamed, and you should ask whether the story is still reliable.
explain: Watch out for phrases like «sources close to», «friends fear» and «everyone says». They often mean one unnamed person, or nobody at all. Unnamed can also describe things: in maths or science, an unnamed point is one you have not labelled yet.
fact: Unnamed means without a name, or whose name is not given.
fact: In news, an unnamed person is someone whose name the reporter does not tell, often to protect them.
fact: «Remain unnamed» means stay without a name, or stay secret.
fact: «Everyone says so» in a gossip story usually means one unnamed person said it.
fact: First reports are often unclear or wrong, so check more than one source before you believe an unnamed source.
exercise: «The reporter wrote about an unnamed man who saw the crash.» What does unnamed mean here? | A man who was not at the crash | A man who is very tall | A man who wears a hat | *A man whose name the reporter does not give
exercise: «Everyone says so» in a gossip story usually means what? | The story is definitely true | *One unnamed person said it | Everybody in the whole town said it | It was said by a named teacher
exercise: Which phrase tells you that a person is kept secret? | *«an unnamed source» | «a named spokesperson» | «the headline is short» | «breaking news»
test: In maths, what does an unnamed point mean? | A point that is always in the middle | *A point you have not labelled yet | A point that has been deleted for ever | A point that is very far away
test: Why might a reporter keep a person unnamed? | *To protect the person | To make the story longer | Because the name is too hard to spell | Because the person asked for a prize
exam: What is the best check before you believe a story with «an unnamed source»? | Believe the headline straight away because it is breaking news | Ignore who is speaking and just read the pictures | *Check more than one source and read past the headline | Share it at once so your friends know
